nodecode-skill-court is a rewrite of saatvik333's saatvik333/hermes-skill-court:
https://github.com/saatvik333/hermes-skill-court
commit de53842858cf41a1437eda30ff553d21cbf578f2

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Saatvik Sharma (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the paired with/without trial and everything the original's
scoring.py decides -- each case is one task run in both arms, repeated pairs
of one case are averaged per case first, every interval is a bootstrap over
cases with a fixed seed, a verdict needs min_tasks cases judged in both arms
and its whole 95% interval on one side, and the token rules that call a
skill harmful when it costs more than `margin' without lifting success and
helpful when it lifts success while costing at least a fifth less. Also the
four verbs the model works the court with (a case added, a pair recorded, a
report read, a skill quarantined or released), the leaderboard the report
prints best first, the quarantine list that hides a skill without editing
it, and the report's own sentence on what the numbers did. The original's
SQLite store is one readable file under the home, written atomically; its
typed settings, defaults and thresholds are the cell's config section.

Left out: the Docker replay itself (a cloned Hermes profile per arm, a
container per run, a shell check inside it), task mining from session
history, the blind LLM judge and its injection-hardened prompt, the appeal
that drafts and proves a fix to a skill's own text, and the automatic
trial queue with its daily budget. A Nodecode cell runs no agent turn of its
own, so the runs are the caller's -- the agent in the session, or an
operator from /skill-court -- and the cell keeps the evidence, decides the
verdict and holds the quarantine. Hiding is a standing notice naming the
skill rather than a filter on its file, because a skill this image keeps is
a definition, not a document on disk.
